¶
'''3.''' To cause to bend in submission; cause to submit; subdue.
{{smaller block|''Bow'' not mine honour.
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''), Two Noble Kinsmen, iii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Authority forgets a dying king,
Laid widow’d of the power in his eye
That ''bow’d'' the will. ''Tennyson'', Morte d’Arthur.</poem>}}

'''4†.''' To bend; inflect; cause to deviate from a given condition.
{{smaller block|We ''bow'' things the contrary way to make them come to their natural straightness.
{{right|''Bacon'', Atheism.}}}}

'''5†.''' To incline; turn in a particular direction; influence.
{{smaller block|Not to ''bow'' and bias their opinions.
{{right|''Fuller''.}}}}
{{smaller block|For troubles and adversities do more ''bow'' men’s minds to religion.
{{right|''Bacon''.}}}}

'''6.''' To bend or incline in worship or adoration, or in token of submission, homage, respect, civility, condescension, or attention.
{{smaller block|And they cried before him, ''Bow'' the knee; and he made him ruler over all the land of Egypt.
{{right|Gen. xli. 43.}}}}
{{smaller block|And Moses made haste, and ''bowed'' his head toward the earth.
{{right|Ex. xxxiv. 8.}}}}
{{smaller block|They came to meet him, and ''bowed'' themselves to the ground before him.
{{right|2 Ki. ii. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Bow'' down thine ear, and hear the words of the wise.
{{right|Prov. xxii. 17.}}}}

'''7.''' To express by a bow or by bowing: as, to ''bow'' one’s thanks or assent.—'''8.''' To accompany or usher in, out, etc., with a bow or bows.
{{smaller block|I saw the station-master ''bow'' them into the carriage.
{{right|''Dickens''.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ancient Hospitality, long since,
With ceremonious thrift, ''bowed'' out of doors.</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', Under the Willows.}}}}

